   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "Equivalent circuit and stability"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Electrically a vibrating crystal is a series R, L, C (representing friction, mass and stiffness) in parallel with the mounting capacitance $C_m$ of the electrodes. It has a series resonant frequency $f_s = 1/(2\\pi\\sqrt{LC})$, where its impedance is minimum, and a parallel resonant frequency $f_p$ just above it, where its impedance is maximum. The Q of a crystal is very high (10⁴ to 10⁶), so the frequency hardly drifts. In a crystal oscillator, such as a Pierce circuit, the crystal replaces the coil of a Colpitts oscillator. Uses: watches, computer clocks, radio transmitters and receivers."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "Series R-L-C in parallel with Cm"
    },
    {
     "en": "fs: minimum impedance; fp slightly higher: maximum"
    },
    {
     "en": "Very high Q: excellent frequency stability"
    },
    {
     "en": "Uses: watches, clocks, transmitters"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "A crystal behaves like a series RLC circuit with the mounting capacitance across it. It has two close resonances and a very high Q, so its frequency is extremely stable.",
    "kn": "ಸ್ಫಟಿಕವು ಮೌಂಟಿಂಗ್ ಧಾರಕತೆ ಸಹಿತ ಸರಣಿ RLC ಮಂಡಲದಂತೆ ವರ್ತಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. ಎರಡು ಹತ್ತಿರದ ಅನುರಣನಗಳು ಮತ್ತು ಅತಿ ಹೆಚ್ಚು Q ಇರುವುದರಿಂದ ಆವೃತ್ತಿ ಅತ್ಯಂತ ಸ್ಥಿರ."
   },
   "formula": "f_s = \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{LC}}, \\qquad f_p = \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{L\\,\\frac{C\\,C_m}{C + C_m}}}"
  },
